Jorge, it is already the case that conversion with SE can change meanings
in the presence of quantified variables, since variables are quantified
in the order of surface appearance:

1)      ro prenu cu prami pa finpe
        all persons love one fish

and

2)      pa finpe cu se prami ro prenu
        one fish is-loved-by all persons

are equivalent respectively to

3)      ro da poi prenu pa de poi finpe zo'u da prami de
        for-all x's which are-persons, there-is-one y which is-a-fish,
                such-that x loves y

and

4)      pa de poi finpe ro da poi prenu zo'u de se prami da
        there-is-one y which is-a-fish for-all x's which are-persons such-that
                y is-loved-by x

and the latter is equivalent in turn to:

5)      pa de poi finpe ro da poi prenu zo'u da prami de

which is not equivalent to Ex. 3, so Ex. 1 and 2 are not equivalent either.
This will be explained properly in the predicate logic paper.

